e</span><span class="enc-snip">Carthaginian Iberia was the territory in southern and eastern Spain ruled by Carthage under the Barcid family from 237 BCE, when Hamilcar Barca landed at Gades, until…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/carthago-delenda-est"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/carthago-delenda-est.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Carthago delenda est</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Carthago delenda est, &quot;Carthage must be destroyed&quot;, is a Latin phrase associated with the Roman senator Cato the Elder, who is said to have ended his speeches with it.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/carthago-nova.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Carthago Nova</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Carthago Nova, modern Cartagena in south-east Spain, was founded around 227 BCE by the Carthaginian general Hasdrubal the Fair as the capital of Barcid Iberia, with a…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cartimandua"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cartimandua.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cartimandua</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cartimandua was queen of the Brigantes of northern Britain in the 1st century CE and a client ruler of Rome.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cartography"><span class="enc-name">Cartography</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Cartography is the science and art of making maps.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/carus-of-segeda"><span class="enc-name">Carus of Segeda</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Carus of Segeda was a Celtiberian leader chosen by the Belli and allied peoples as their general in 153 BCE, when Rome attacked over the fortification of Segeda.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/casa-da-india"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/casa-da-india.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Casa da Índia</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Casa da Índia was the royal trading house in Lisbon that managed the crown monopoly over spice imports from Asia in the sixteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Casa de Contratación</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Casa de Contratación, or House of Trade, was the Spanish crown agency founded in Seville in 1503 to regulate trade and navigation with the Americas.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-la-vall"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/casa-de-la-vall.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Casa de la Vall</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Casa de la Vall is a stone manor house in Andorra la Vella, built in 1580 as the residence of the Busquets family and acquired in 1702 by the Consell General as its…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/casablanca"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/casablanca.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Casablanca</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Casablanca is the largest city of Morocco and its chief port, on the Atlantic coast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/casale-monferrato"><img cla