humb" src="/images/encyclopedia/casimir-i-of-opole.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Casimir I of Opole</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Casimir I of Opole (c. 1178–1230) was a Piast duke of Opole and Racibórz in Upper Silesia from 1211, a son of Mieszko IV Tanglefoot.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/casimir-ii-of-leczyca"><span class="enc-name">Casimir II of Łęczyca</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Casimir II of Łęczyca (c. 1261–1294) was a Polish prince of the Piast dynasty, a son of Casimir I of Kuyavia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/casimir-ii-the-just"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/casimir-ii-the-just.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Casimir II the Just</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Casimir II the Just was a Piast duke, the youngest son of Bolesław III Wrymouth.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Casimir III the Great</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Casimir III the Great was King of Poland from 1333 to 1370, the last of the Piast kings.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Casimir IV Jagiellon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Casimir IV Jagiellon was Grand Duke of Lithuania from 1440 and King of Poland from 1447.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/casita-volcano"><span class="enc-name">Casita (volcano)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Casita is a volcano in north-western Nicaragua, part of the San Cristóbal volcanic complex near Chinandega.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/caslav-klonimirovic"><span class="enc-name">Časlav Klonimirović</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Časlav Klonimirović was Prince of Serbia in the 10th century and the last ruler of the Vlastimirović dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/caspe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/caspe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Caspe</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Caspe is a town in the province of Zaragoza in Aragon, north-eastern Spain, near the Ebro River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/caspian-flotilla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/caspian-flotilla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Caspian Flotilla</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Caspian Flotilla is the Russian naval force on the Caspian Sea, founded under Peter the Great in 1722.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/caspian-sea.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Caspian Sea</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Caspian Sea is the world's largest inland body of water, between the Caucasus, Iran and Central Asia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cassander"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cassander.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cassander</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cassander was a Macedonian general and ruler of Macedonia after the death of Alexander the 